  ))}

  <h2 class="section">Days of volume — the column that matters most</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    An unlock is only meaningful against the liquidity that has to absorb it. Ten million tokens
    into a market trading a hundred million a day is noise; the same ten million into a market
    trading two million a day is a week of selling pressure. That ratio is the column above:
    tokens × mark price, divided by 24-hour traded volume, both from the same snapshot every
    other page here reads.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    It fills only where this site already carries a perpetual for the token, because that is the
    only volume series it trusts. Raising the published set to
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/funding">{snap.perps.length || 50} coins</a> is what put CRV and AAVE
    on both sides of that gap. For anything else the cell stays blank rather than borrowing a
    number from a source the rest of the page would not accept.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">How to read the provenance badges</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="max-width:860px">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Provenance</th><th>What it means</th><th class="num">Rows</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {reg.provenanceScale.map((p: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td><span class:list={["prov", `prov--${p.key}`]}>{p.label}</span></td>
            <td class="reason">{p.meaning}</td>
            <td class="num">{p.key === "contract" ? contractCount : 0}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note">
    {/* "FOR ITS ON-CHAIN FIGURES", NOT "FOR ITS FIGURES". Each row also carries a USD value and
        days of volume to absorb, which come from the exchange snapshot, not from an eth_call — the
        wider wording covered numbers this provenance does not describe. */}
    Every row on this page carries the strongest provenance for its on-chain figures. That is not a boast — it is what
    happens when the weaker sources are unavailable, and it is why the register is {contractCount} contracts
    deep rather than several hundred wide.{addressInferred.length > 0 && <>{" "}{addressInferred.length === 1 ? "One row is weaker on a different question, and says so in its own Provenance cell" : `${addressInferred.length} rows are weaker on a different question, and say so in their own Provenance cells`}:
    the {`${addressInferred.map((s) => s.symbol).join(" and ")} ${addressInferred.length === 1 ? "address was" : "addresses were"}`} identified by reading the chain rather than from a document, which is a weaker
    claim about the ADDRESS than the other rows make, and it is said out loud rather than hidden.</>}
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Contract-verified emission detail</h2>
  {emitting.map(({ e, f }) => {
    /* The detail table uses the same `forward` as the Releasing now row, so a horizon cannot be
       capped in one table and uncapped in the other. `byAllowance` and `byStep` are the horizons
       each kind of limit reaches, and each sentence under the table names exactly its own rows.
       A lapsed contract gets no table: every row would be zero, and the reason is the sentence.
       The epoch length is the distance between two readings, start_epoch_time() and
       future_epoch_time_write(), not the "365-day" the prose and a typed epochLengthDays both
       carried (verifier, 16 September 2026: two sources for one fact, neither of them a getter). */
    const rows = HORIZONS.map((h) => ({ h, ...f.over(h) }));
    const byAllowance = rows.filter((r) => r.limit === "allowance").map((r) => r.h);
    const byStep = rows.filter((r) => r.limit === "step").map((r) => r.h);
    const singular = (hs: number[]) => hs.length === 1 || hs.length === HORIZONS.length;
    const epochDays = e.epochStart && e.epochEnd ? Math.round((d(e.epochEnd) - d(e.epochStart)) / DAY) : null;
    const epochPhrase = e.miningEpoch
      ? ` (epoch ${e.miningEpoch}${epochDays ? `, ${epochDays} days from ${fmt(e.epochStart)} to ${fmt(e.epochEnd)}` : ""})`
      : "";
    const allowanceCut = !f.lapsed && byAllowance.length > 0
      ? `, and ${rowsPhrase(byAllowance)} ${singular(byAllowance) ? "counts" : "count"} nothing past that date`
      : "";
    const allowanceSo = f.lapsed ? "the contract may still be paying, and no forward total is printed until the register is re-read"
      : byAllowance.length > 0 ? `${rowsPhrase(byAllowance)} ${singular(byAllowance) ? "is" : "are"} what the last reading covers, not a ceiling`
      : "only a re-read says how much of it is left";
    return (
    <>
      <p class="prose">
        <strong>{e.name}</strong> runs on a schedule the contract itself publishes:
        {" "}{`${e.ratePerSecond.toFixed(6)} ${e.symbol}`} per second, which is
        {" "}<strong>{`${num(e.perDay)} ${e.symbol} a day`}</strong> — {`${e.mechanism}${epochPhrase}`}. {e.detail}{" "}{fill(e.note ?? "", {
          supply: num(e.totalSupply),
          endYear: e.distributionEnd ? String(new Date(`${e.distributionEnd}T00:00:00Z`).getUTCFullYear()) : "",
        })}
      </p>
      {!f.lapsed && (
      <div class="panel" style="max-width:640px">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th class="num">Next</th><th class="num">{f.fundedUntil !== null ? `${e.symbol} the read allowance covers` : `${e.symbol} emitted`}</th><th class="num">Share of total supply</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <tr class={r.h === horizon ? "is-active" : undefined}>
                <td class="num">{r.h} days</td>
                <td class="num">{r.limit === "step" ? "up to " : ""}{num(r.tokens)}</td>
                <td class="num">{r.limit === "step" ? "up to " : ""}{pct(r.tokens / e.totalSupply)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      )}
      <p class="section-note">
        Read from <code class="addr">{e.contract}</code> at block {reg.verifiedBlock.toLocaleString("en-US")} using
        {" "}{e.getters.map((g: string, i: number) => (<><code>{g}</code>{i < e.getters.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>))}.
        Available supply {num(e.availableSupply)}, total supply {num(e.totalSupply)}, both read on-chain.
        {f.fundedUntil !== null && (
          <>{" "}At that block the vault was approved to spend <b>{`${num(e.fundedTokens)} ${e.symbol}`}</b>, {Math.round(e.fundedTokens / e.perDay)} days
          at this rate. Drawn at exactly that rate from the read, it {`${f.lapsed ? "would have been spent by" : "is spent on"} ${fmtTs(f.fundedUntil)}${allowanceCut}`}.
          That date is an assumption, not a reading: claims can lag what has accrued and governance can raise the allowance, so {allowanceSo}.
          {" "}<code>DISTRIBUTION_END</code>{" "}reads {fmt(e.distributionEnd)}, which is a sentinel, not a plan.</>
        )}
        {f.stepAt !== null && (
          <>{" "}<code>rate()</code>{" "}steps down when epoch {e.miningEpoch} ends on {fmtTs(f.stepAt)}{byStep.length > 0 ? `; ${rowsPhrase(byStep)} ${singular(byStep) ? "runs" : "run"} past that date, so ${byStep.length === 1 ? "it is" : byStep.length === HORIZONS.length ? "each is" : "they are"} an upper bound \u2014 the rate after the step is lower, and this register has not read it` : ""}.</>
        )}
      </p>
    </>
    );
  })}

  <h2 class="section">Addresses that carry no readable schedule</h2>
  <p class="section-note">
    Named in project material as allocations, but exposing nothing to read. Listed because the
    absence is the finding: a balance with no schedule is not the same as no balance, and it is
    not the same as a balance with a date either.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th class="num">Held now</th><th>Why it has no row above</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {reg.unverified.map((u: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td style="white-space:nowrap">{u.label}<br /><a class="addr" href={`https://etherscan.io/address/${u.address}#readContract`} rel="noopener" target="_blank"><code>{u.address}</code></a></td>
            <td class="num">{u.heldNow === null ? <span class="dim" title="Each recipient's schedule is a separate clone; enumerating them needs an archival endpoint">not enumerable</span> : `${num(u.heldNow)} ${u.symbol}`}</td>
            <td class="reason">{u.reason}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section" id="filling">What would extend the register</h2>
  <div class="verdict verdict--ok">
    <div>
      <b>The register is deep rather than wide, and that is a deliberate trade rather than a
      shortfall.</b> Every row was read from a contract by hand. The dataset that would make it
      wide in one step is DefiLlama's emissions feed, and <code>api.llama.fi/emissions</code>
      {" "}answers <b>402 Payment Required — "Upgrade to the paid API plan"</b>. Their adapters
      repository is not public, and the one that is carries no licence at all, which under default
      copyright grants nothing. A public storage bucket does serve the same data, but reaching for
      it to route around a 402 is circumventing an access control, not accepting an offer. So
      breadth is a purchase decision. Depth — reading each contract's balance rather than its
      published schedule — is what produced the finished-but-unwithdrawn rows above, and it costs
      only time. Whether anyone else publishes them is not something this site has measured, so
      it is not claimed.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="max-width:900px">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Cost</th><th>What it yields</th><th class="num">Provenance it earns</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>DefiLlama Pro</strong></td>
          <td class="reason">A paid API subscription. Their emissions endpoint is explicitly gated behind it.</td>
          <td class="reason">Several hundred protocols at once, with forward dates. The fastest route to breadth by a wide margin — and forward dates only, so it would not have found a single row in the overhang section above.</td>
          <td class="num">Aggregator-sourced</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="is-active">
          <td><strong>Manual curation</strong></td>
          <td class="reason">Roughly 15–25 minutes per contract to find a primary source for the address, derive the getters and read it, at about a one-in-three hit rate.</td>
          <td class="reason">{curationYield}</td>
          <td class="num">Contract-verified</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Archival RPC</strong></td>
          <td class="reason">An endpoint with unrestricted <code>eth_getLogs</code>. Free providers cap it at 50 blocks, about ten minutes of chain.</td>
          <td class="reason">Every factory-deployed escrow in one pass — the Lido case above and everything shaped like it.</td>
          <td class="num">Contract-verified</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="prose">
    The three are complementary rather than alternatives. Buying the aggregator feed adds breadth
    immediately at the weakest provenance; the archival endpoint reaches contracts that exist only
    as factory clones; curation is what reads a balance and notices that a finished schedule never
    emptied. The badge column is what makes running all three at once coherent instead of muddled.
  </p>

